# Response headers for anything served as a static asset.
#
# Cloudflare parses this file and applies its rules; it is never served itself.
# It has to sit at the root of the assets directory, which is build/web — so it
# lives here in site/, and tool/build_web.dart copies site/ over the built
# client. See docs/cloudflare-runbook.md.
#
# This replaces firebase.json's `headers` block and is a third of its length,
# because eleven of its fourteen rules were spelling out, one path at a time,
# what Workers already does to every asset: `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate` with an ETag, so a service worker script, an index.html or a
# manifest is revalidated on every load and can never be served stale. The
# rules below are the ones that say something the default does not.
#
# One caution worth stating, because it is invisible until it bites: these
# reach static assets only. Responses the Worker generates — the whole of
# /api/* — are not touched by this file even when the path matches. What the
# API asserts about itself, it asserts in Hono.
#
# The app shell *is* covered, though, including on a cold deep link. Those are
# served by the Worker calling env.ASSETS.fetch(), and the asset router applies
# these rules before handing the response back — verified, because the opposite
# would mean cross-origin isolation depended on how somebody arrived at a page.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()

# Cross-origin isolation, scoped to the client rather than to the origin.
#
# It is what lets sqlite3.wasm use SharedArrayBuffer, which is what makes the
# local-first database work in a browser at all. It is also what stops a page
# loading anything cross-origin that has not opted in — a real constraint, and
# a pointless one to impose on marketing pages at the root that somebody else
# designs and that embed Google Fonts.
#
# The pair is safe here only because no sign-in depends on a popup any more:
# the web build hands the whole page to Google and comes back. Reinstating an
# in-page Google button without first removing these breaks sign-in silently,
# in release builds only.
/app/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless
